---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias31.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Three Forms of Joint Venture" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Jointly Controlled Operations" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Jointly Controlled Assets" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Jointly Controlled Entities" },
        { id: "s7", label: "The Equity Method Alternative" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Transactions With a Joint Venture" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IAS 31 vs IFRS 11" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="superseded"
    explanation="IAS 31 was withdrawn and replaced by IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements (together with IFRS 10 and the revised IAS 28), effective for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2013."
    historical="It is retained here for historical reference and because its proportionate consolidation vs equity method distinction remains a useful conceptual bridge to IFRS 11's joint operation/joint venture split. For current joint arrangement accounting, always apply IFRS 11."
    link="/ifrs/ifrs11"
    linkText="IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why joint control needed its own recognition logic</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework's control-based consolidation logic works cleanly when ONE party controls an investee. Joint control — where NO single party controls, but a defined group of parties together do, requiring unanimous consent — needed a DIFFERENT mechanism to faithfully represent each venturer's genuine share of the arrangement's resources and obligations.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 31 MAPPED TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Substance over form</strong> — IAS 31 classified joint ventures by their ECONOMIC STRUCTURE (operations, assets, or entities) rather than by legal form alone, since the appropriate accounting genuinely differed based on what was actually being jointly controlled</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Faithful representation</strong> — proportionate consolidation (Section 6) aimed to show each venturer's OWN SHARE of the joint venture's assets, liabilities, income, and expenses, directly on its own financial statements — a more granular representation than the single "net investment" line the equity method provides</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Choice as an information trade-off</strong> — offering proportionate consolidation AND the equity method as free alternatives reflected differing views on which better served users, a debate IFRS 11 later resolved by choice restriction</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 31's genuine POLICY CHOICE between proportionate consolidation and the equity method for jointly controlled entities was itself controversial — critics argued it reduced comparability between entities making different (equally valid) elections for economically similar arrangements, a key driver behind IFRS 11's reform.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IAS 31</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 31's THREE-way structural classification drove fundamentally different measurement treatments, unlike the single equity-method approach IFRS 11 later adopted for joint ventures.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Structure</span><span>Measurement approach</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Jointly controlled operations</span><span>Venturer recognises its OWN assets, liabilities, expenses, and share of income directly — no separate entity exists</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Jointly controlled assets</span><span>Venturer recognises its SHARE of the jointly controlled assets and any liabilities it incurs directly</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Jointly controlled entities</span><span>Venturer's CHOICE: proportionate consolidation OR equity method</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ <strong style="font-weight:500">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> allowing a genuine choice for jointly controlled entities traded off <strong style="font-weight:500">comparability</strong> (different entities could report the SAME economic arrangement differently) for greater PREPARER flexibility — ultimately judged by IFRS 11 to tilt too far away from comparability.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 31 covered</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 31 applied to accounting for interests in joint ventures, and the reporting of joint venture assets, liabilities, income, and expenses in the financial statements of venturers and investors, REGARDLESS of the structures or forms under which the joint venture activities took place.</div>
        <div class="note">This general applicability, and the underlying "joint control" concept itself, was carried forward into IFRS 11 — the core CONTROL threshold definition did not fundamentally change; only the accounting CONSEQUENCE of that classification did.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Joint venture</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A contractual arrangement whereby two or more parties undertake an economic activity that is subject to JOINT CONTROL.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Joint control</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The contractually agreed sharing of control over an economic activity, existing only when the strategic financial and operating decisions relating to the activity require the UNANIMOUS consent of the parties sharing control.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Venturer</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A party to a joint venture that has joint control over that joint venture.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Three Forms of Joint Venture</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Classification by economic structure, not just legal form</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 31 identified THREE distinct structural forms a joint venture could take, each with its own accounting treatment — a critical distinction from IFRS 11's later binary classification (joint operation vs joint venture).</div>
        <div class="three-col">
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-info);">1. Jointly controlled operations</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Involves the use of the venturers' OWN assets rather than establishing a separate entity — e.g. combining manufacturing/marketing resources under a contractual arrangement to share output/revenue/costs.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-info);">2. Jointly controlled assets</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Involves JOINT CONTROL, and often joint OWNERSHIP, of one or more assets contributed to/acquired for the joint venture — e.g. a jointly owned oil pipeline, with each venturer taking a share of output.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-info);">3. Jointly controlled entities</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Involves the establishment of a CORPORATION, PARTNERSHIP, or other entity in which each venturer has an interest — the entity itself owns the assets, incurs liabilities, and controls the economic activity.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Operations (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">No separate accounting records for the joint venture itself</div>
        <div class="principle">A venturer recognises, in respect of its interest in a jointly controlled operation, in its OWN financial statements: the ASSETS it controls, the LIABILITIES it incurs, the EXPENSES it incurs, and its SHARE of the income earned from the sale of goods/services by the joint venture — no separate accounting records for the joint venture itself are needed.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Two manufacturers jointly marketing a product</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            Two companies agree to jointly manufacture and market a product, each contributing their own manufacturing facilities and bearing their own respective costs, sharing REVENUE from combined sales 50/50 per the contractual arrangement. Company A's own manufacturing costs for the period: $400,000. Total combined sales revenue: $1,200,000.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Company A recognises its OWN assets (its manufacturing facility) and its OWN expenses ($400,000) directly in its own financial statements, PLUS its contractual share of revenue = 1,200,000 × 50% = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$600,000</strong> — no separate joint venture entity or set of books exists at all.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Recognising a proportionate share directly</div>
        <div class="principle">In respect of its interest in a jointly controlled asset, a venturer recognises, in its own financial statements: its SHARE of the jointly controlled assets, classified according to the asset's nature; any LIABILITIES it has incurred; its SHARE of any liabilities incurred JOINTLY with the other venturers; any INCOME from the sale/use of its share of the joint venture's output, together with its share of any expenses incurred by the joint venture; and any EXPENSES it has incurred in respect of its interest.</div>
        <div class="note">This treatment is essentially "look through" the joint arrangement structure entirely — each venturer accounts for its OWN proportionate slice of assets, liabilities, income, and expenses, without any consolidation mechanics whatsoever.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Entities — Proportionate Consolidation (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The benchmark treatment IFRS 11 abolished</div>
        <div class="principle">Under PROPORTIONATE CONSOLIDATION, a venturer's SOFP included its share of the assets it JOINTLY controlled and its share of the liabilities for which it was jointly responsible; its SOPL included its share of the income and expenses of the jointly controlled entity — LINE BY LINE, combined with similar items in the venturer's own financial statements, or reported as separate line items.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Proportionate consolidation of a 40%-owned jointly controlled entity</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A venturer holds a 40% interest in a jointly controlled entity. The entity's own financial statements show: total assets $5,000,000, total liabilities $2,000,000, revenue $3,000,000, and expenses $2,400,000.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Venturer's share, brought into its own SOFP/SOPL LINE BY LINE:</strong><br>
            Assets: 5,000,000 × 40% = $2,000,000<br>
            Liabilities: 2,000,000 × 40% = $800,000<br>
            Revenue: 3,000,000 × 40% = $1,200,000<br>
            Expenses: 2,400,000 × 40% = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$960,000</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger" style="margin-bottom:0;">✕ <strong style="font-weight:600">This LINE-BY-LINE proportionate combination is the single feature IFRS 11 completely eliminated</strong> for joint ventures (as opposed to joint operations) — joint ventures under IFRS 11 are now accounted for ONLY via the equity method, a single net investment line, never proportionate consolidation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Equity Method Alternative</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The permitted alternative treatment for jointly controlled entities</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 31 permitted the EQUITY METHOD (per IAS 28's mechanics) as an ALTERNATIVE to proportionate consolidation for interests in jointly controlled entities — a genuine, unrestricted policy choice available to ALL venturers, applied consistently to all jointly controlled entity interests.</div>
        <div class="note">This equity-method ALTERNATIVE, once optional under IAS 31, became the ONLY permitted treatment for joint ventures under IFRS 11 — effectively, IFRS 11 removed the proportionate consolidation choice entirely rather than removing the equity method.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Transactions Between a Venturer and a Joint Venture</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Unrealised profit elimination — to the extent of the venturer's own interest</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">When a venturer contributed or sold assets to a joint venture, recognition of any portion of a gain/loss resulting from the transaction reflected the SUBSTANCE of the transaction — the venturer recognised only the portion of the gain/loss ATTRIBUTABLE to the interests of the OTHER venturers, similar in spirit to the IAS 28 equity method's upstream/downstream unrealised profit elimination logic.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GENERAL DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>A list and description of interests in significant joint ventures, and the proportion of ownership interest held</span>
              <span>The method used to recognise interests in jointly controlled entities (proportionate consolidation or equity method)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">AGGREGATE AMOUNTS DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The aggregate amount of current/non-current assets, liabilities, income, and expenses related to interests in jointly controlled entities</span>
              <span>Contingent liabilities and capital commitments in respect of interests in joint ventures</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement mattered most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether genuine JOINT control (requiring unanimous consent) existed, versus control by a single party or mere significant influence</span>
              <span>Classifying the arrangement as a jointly controlled operation, asset, or entity, based on its actual economic structure</span>
              <span>Which measurement policy (proportionate consolidation or equity method) to elect for jointly controlled entities</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Fair value of assets contributed to a jointly controlled entity, for determining gain/loss recognition on contribution</span>
              <span>Allocation of jointly incurred liabilities between venturers under jointly controlled asset arrangements</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ The free CHOICE between proportionate consolidation and the equity method for economically IDENTICAL jointly controlled entities was itself a significant comparability weakness — different venturers in the SAME joint venture could report completely different SOFP/SOPL presentations of the same underlying economic interest.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IAS 31 vs IFRS 11 — What Changed</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — the transition from IAS 31 to IFRS 11</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 31 (superseded)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">IFRS 11 (current)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Classification structure</span><span>THREE forms: jointly controlled operations, assets, and entities</span><span>TWO categories: joint operations and joint ventures, classified by RIGHTS AND OBLIGATIONS rather than legal structure alone</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Jointly controlled entity accounting</span><span>CHOICE: proportionate consolidation OR equity method</span><span>NO choice — joint ventures MUST use the equity method only; proportionate consolidation is entirely ELIMINATED</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Joint operations</span><span>Broadly equivalent to "jointly controlled operations/assets"</span><span>Each party recognises its own share of assets, liabilities, revenue, and expenses directly — very similar in substance to IAS 31's treatment of jointly controlled assets/operations</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Classification driver</span><span>Legal/structural form of the arrangement (operation, asset, or entity)</span><span>The PARTIES' rights and obligations arising from the arrangement (substance-focused, even for a separate vehicle)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The single most important change was the ELIMINATION of proportionate consolidation as an option for joint ventures — IFRS 11 concluded that a single equity-method line better represented a venturer's genuine (non-controlling) interest than gross proportionate line items, resolving IAS 31's comparability weakness by removing the choice entirely.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every Joint Venture Event Landed (Historical, Under IAS 31)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Jointly controlled operation — own assets/expenses</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Recognised directly, as the venturer's own items</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Jointly controlled operation — share of joint income</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Venturer's contractual share recognised directly</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Jointly controlled asset — share recognised</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Classified by the asset's nature, alongside similar owned assets</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Jointly controlled entity — proportionate consolidation</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Share of EVERY line item combined proportionately</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Jointly controlled entity — equity method elected</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Single net investment line, per IAS 28 mechanics</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Contribution of assets to a joint venture</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Gain recognised only to the extent attributable to OTHER venturers' interests</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash flows from the jointly controlled arrangement</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span><span>Proportionate share included if proportionate consolidation was elected</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>List of significant joint ventures, method elected</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 31's legacy connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 11</span><span>The DIRECT successor standard — eliminated proportionate consolidation, mandating the equity method for all joint ventures</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 28</span><span>Supplied the equity method mechanics that were an OPTIONAL alternative under IAS 31, and are now the MANDATORY treatment for joint ventures under IFRS 11</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 10</span><span>Supplied the "control" concept distinguishing subsidiaries (full consolidation) from joint ventures (joint control, no single controller)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 12</span><span>Now supplies the extensive disclosure requirements about interests in joint arrangements, replacing IAS 31's own disclosure section</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 24</span><span>Joint ventures are explicitly identified as related parties of the venturer, requiring transaction and balance disclosures</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Proportionate consolidation", definition: "A method of accounting whereby a venturer's share of each of the assets, liabilities, income, and expenses of a jointly controlled entity is combined LINE BY LINE with similar items in the venturer's own financial statements, or reported as separate line items — now abolished under IFRS 11." },
      { term: "Jointly controlled entity", definition: "A joint venture involving the establishment of a corporation, partnership, or other entity in which each venturer has an interest." },
      { term: "Jointly controlled operations", definition: "A joint venture involving the use of the venturers' own assets and resources, rather than the establishment of a separate entity." },
      { term: "Jointly controlled assets", definition: "A joint venture involving joint control, and often joint ownership, of one or more assets contributed to, or acquired for, the purposes of the joint venture." },
      { term: "Investor in a joint venture", definition: "A party to a joint venture that does NOT have joint control over that joint venture — distinct from a 'venturer,' which does have joint control." },
      { term: "Contractual arrangement", definition: "The agreement (often in writing) establishing joint control, distinguishing a joint venture from mere significant influence over an associate." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
